13: {&quot;step_index&quot;:13,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;SYSTEM&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;EPHEMERAL_MESSAGE&quot;,&quot;status&quot;:&quot;DONE&quot;,&quot;created_at&quot;:&quot;2026-07-26T08:13:31Z&quot;,&quot;content&quot;:&quot;The following is an &lt;EPHEMERAL_MESSAGE&gt; not actually sent by the user. It is provided by the system as a set of reminders and general important information to pay attention to. Do NOT respond to this message, just act accordingly.\n\n&lt;EPHEMERAL_MESSAGE&gt;\n&lt;planning_mode&gt;\nYou&apos;re in planning mode. Exercise judgement on whether the user&apos;s last request warrants a plan and take action accordingly.\nIf the user&apos;s request warrants a plan then follow this workflow:\n1. Research first before taking actions. Do not use your code editing tools or run modifying commands during this time.\n2. Create an implementation plan artifact with `request_feedback = true` to request approval. Include any open questions for the user directly in the implementation plan. Don&apos;t use the ask_question tool to ask these questions. Stop calling tools and allow the user to review the plan and provide feedback or approval.\n3. After receiving approval, begin execution. Create and update the task artifact as you work.\n4. Verify any work that you have done.\n5. Create a walkthrough artifact to present your work to the user. When writing artifacts, make sure to follow the artifact formatting guidelines and use github alerts to highlight critical sections.\nIf the user&apos;s request does NOT warrant a plan then continue your work WITHOUT making a plan or requesting user feedback.\n&lt;/planning_mode&gt;\n&lt;bash_command_reminder&gt;\nCRITICAL INSTRUCTION 1: You may have access to a variety of tools at your disposal. Some tools may be for a specific task such as &apos;view_file&apos; (for viewing contents of a file). Others may be very broadly applicable such as the ability to run a command on a terminal. Always prioritize using the most specific tool you can for the task at hand. Here are some rules: (a) NEVER run cat inside a bash command to create a new file or append to an existing file. (b) ALWAYS use grep_search instead of running grep inside a bash command unless absolutely needed. (c) DO NOT use ls for listing, cat for viewing, grep for finding, sed for replacing. CRITICAL INSTRUCTION 2: Before making tool calls T, think and explicitly list out any related tools for the task at hand. You can only execute a set of tools T if all other tools in the list are either more generic or cannot be used for the task at hand. ALWAYS START your thought with recalling critical instructions 1 and 2. In particular, the format for the start of your thought block must be &apos;...94&gt;thought\nCRITICAL INSTRUCTION 1: ...\nCRITICAL INSTRUCTION 2: ...&apos;.\n&lt;/bash_command_reminder&gt;\n&lt;/EPHEMERAL_MESSAGE&gt;&quot;}
14: {&quot;step_index&quot;:14,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;MODEL&quot;,&quot;type&quot;:&quot;PLANNER_RESPONSE&quot;,&quot;status&quot;:&quot;DONE&quot;,&quot;created_at&quot;:&quot;2026-07-26T08:13:31Z&quot;,&quot;thinking&quot;:&quot;**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI&apos;m focusing on tool selection now. The priority is clear: specific tools first. I&apos;m reminding myself to avoid general tools when a dedicated option is available, particularly avoiding `cat` inside bash commands. It’s a crucial distinction for efficiency and safety.\n\n\n**Refining Checklist Categories**\n\nI&apos;m now diving into the MECE security checklist details. I&apos;m fleshing out categories, starting with AuthN/AuthZ. I&apos;ve begun listing specific items under this category, such as preventing non-member session hijacking via HttpOnly cookies and session expiration, along with access control for other&apos;s interpretations, using Row-Level Security (RLS) policies, and double-checking ownership upon client access.\n\n\n**Detailing Security Measures**\n\nI&apos;m now focusing on specific security aspects. I&apos;ve broken it down further, looking at defense against unauthorized admin privilege escalation. I&apos;m also examining how to prevent non-member password plaintext storage, by ensuring proper hash application. Moreover, I&apos;m verifying data protection with PII filtering and XSS prevention, alongside robust database access control and RLS policy checks. I&apos;m also concentrating on business logic, ensuring payment integrity.\n\n\n&quot;,&quot;tool_calls&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;call_mcp_tool&quot;,&quot;args&quot;:{&quot;Arguments&quot;:&quot;{\&quot;nextThoughtNeeded\&quot;:true,\&quot;thought\&quot;:\&quot;MECE 카테고리별로 구체적인 체크리스트를 도출합니다.\\n1. 인증 및 인가 (AuthN &amp; AuthZ): 비회원 세션 쿠키 탈취 방어(HttpOnly, Secure 적용 여부), 타인 주문 및 해몽 결과 무단 열람 방어(RLS 정책 완결성, 서버 단 소유권 이중 검증), 관리자 권한 강제 승격 및 API 무단 호출 방어(보안키 검증 로직, 미들웨어/서버 액션 인가 처리).\\n2. 데이터 보호 (Data Protection): 비회원 비밀번호 단방향 암호화 저장, 유저 입력 꿈 텍스트 내 개인정보(PII) 수집 최소화 및 XSS/SQL Injection 필터링, 전체 테이블 RLS 활성화 및 민감 데이터(결제 Raw 데이터 등) 접근 차단.\\n3. 결제 및 비즈니스 로직 (Payment &amp; Business Logic): 결제 요청 시 클라이언트 전달 금액 위변조 방어(서버 정찰가 비교 및 토스페이먼츠 이중 검증), 다회권 사용 시 동시성 이슈로 인한 중복 차감 방어(DB 트랜잭션, 낙관적/비관적 락 활용), 환불 남용 방어(이미 분석 시작/완료된 건에 대한 환불 차단).\\n4. 외부 API 연동 (External API &amp; AI): Gemini API 프롬프트 인젝션 방어, AI 이미지 생성 API(Pollinations) 호출 시 유저 입력 텍스트 정제(URL 인코딩 및 유해 단어 필터링).\\n5. 인프라 및 보안 설정 (Infrastructure &amp; Config): .env 환경변수 노출 방지(NEXT_PUBLIC_ 구분 철저), 비회원 로그인/관리자 인증 등 중요 폼의 Rate Limiting 적용(무차별 대입 공격 방어).\&quot;,\&quot;thoughtNumber\&quot;:2,\&quot;totalThoughts\&quot;:4}&quot;,&quot;ServerName&quot;:&quot;\&quot;sequential-thinking\&quot;&quot;,&quot;ToolName&quot;:&quot;\&quot;sequentialthinking\&quot;&quot;,&quot;toolAction&quot;:&quot;\&quot;Using sequential thinking\&quot;&quot;,&quot;toolSummary&quot;:&quot;\&quot;Generate MECE categories\&quot;&quot;}}]}
